6.12 Writing Functions
Functions in R allow you to encapsulate operations and reuse code. Define a function using function():
increment &lt;- function(x) {
  x + 1  # Adds 1 to the input value and returns it
}

increment(3)  # Returns 4
Note that functions always return the last value computed:
increment &lt;- function(x) {
  x + 2
  x + 1
}

increment(3)  # Returns 4
Functions can also take multiple arguments:
add_numbers &lt;- function(x, y) {
  return(x + y)
}

add_numbers(2, 5)  # Returns 7
A common example is a function to calculate the hypotenuse of a right triangle (you will use this daily):
hypotenuse &lt;- function(a, b) {
  sqrt(a^2 + b^2)  # Uses the Pythagorean theorem
}

hypotenuse(3, 4)  # Returns 5

Logical operators are able to check whether certain conditions are TRUE or FALSE. The standard logical operators remain fairly consistent with other programs for operators such as ==, !=, &lt;, &lt;=, &gt;, &gt;=; however, the And and Or operators are provided by & and | respectively. This could differ from previously learnt languages. && and || are also logical operators available.
As you might expect, there are subtle differences between these latter operators. To see the difference, consider & and &&.

a &lt;- c(1,2,5)
b &lt;- a

HecticCalculation &lt;- function(){

  print('I am doing a hectic calculation, I promise')

  return(T)
}

# Operator comparison

if(all(a != b) & HecticCalculation()){
  print('I should be unreachable, first condition false')
}

[1] "I am doing a hectic calculation, I promise"



if(all(a != b) && HecticCalculation()){
  print('I should be unreachable, first condition false')
}


if(all(a == b) && HecticCalculation()){
  print('I can run.')
}

[1] "I am doing a hectic calculation, I promise"
[1] "I can run."


The first if statement runs the hectic calculation because it checks both of the conditions on either side of it!
The && does not run the hectic calculation for the second if statement because the first condition fails (it prints nothing). For final if statement runs the hectic calculation, since the first condition passed, therefore the second condition is checked.
This type of logical behaviour can be very useful when dealing with difficult computations if used effectively, avoiding unnecessary function calls. The same can be achieved with ||.
In the above code, we have used the if statement which runs code if a condition is met. With this, we have the else and if else statements. They generally follow a structure like this:

aCondition = F
anotherCondition = T

if (aCondition){
  "Check original condition"
}else if (anotherCondition){
  "Check another condition IF `aCondition` is false"
}else {
  "If all of the above are false, then do this"
}

[1] "Check another condition IF `aCondition` is false"
